{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>בְּמִסְפַּר etc. after the Jubilee.</b> One must know: first, why did it say ״אַחַר הַיּוֹבֵל״ and not say ״עַד הַיּוֹבֵל״, for the years being purchased are from the time of purchase until the Jubilee, not what has elapsed from the Jubilee year until the time of purchase. Second, why did the verse change its language: when it mentioned the number of years it said ״תִּקְנֶה״, attributing the matter to the buyer, whereas when it mentioned the number of harvests it said ״יִמְכָּר לָךְ״, attributing the matter to the seller." "And in tractate Arakhin, chapter HaMokher (Arakhin 29b), they taught: One who sells his field at the time of the Jubilee is not permitted to redeem it in less than two years, as it is said ״בְּמִסְפַּר שְׁנֵי תְבוּאוֹת״. If it was a year of blight or the Sabbatical year, it does not count toward the tally. Rabbi Eliezer says: If he sold it to him at the beginning of the year fully loaded with produce, he eats three harvests, end of quote. And in the Gemara: ״אֵינוֹ גּוֹאֵל״ it did not teach, but rather ״אֵינוֹ מוּתָּר״ — hence he holds there is also a prohibition. And this is not difficult regarding the seller, who stands under a positive commandment, as it is written ״יִמְכָּר לָךְ״; but even the buyer too stands under a positive commandment, as it is written ״תִּקְנֶה״, end of quote. It is further taught there in this language: If he consumed one year before the Jubilee, they complete for him another year after the Jubilee, end of quote. We thus find four halakhot: first — it is a commandment for both of them that the sale not be less than the limit stated in the verse; second — that if there were three harvests in two years, they belong to the buyer; third — that if there were no harvests in the two years, such as a year of blight etc., it does not count toward his tally; fourth — that if the Jubilee occurred within the two years, it does not go out until they complete two years. And the Rambam ruled these four halakhot in chapter 11 of the Laws of Shemittah and Yovel." "<b>And on</b> this basis the fine points will be resolved properly. The verse's saying <b>בְּמִסְפַּר שָׁנִים אַחַר הַיּוֹבֵל</b> means — the number of years that the verse says must be fulfilled, even though they may be completed after the Jubilee, for the Jubilee does not void them. And the verse changed its language, saying once ״תִּקְנֶה״ and once ״יִמְכָּר״, to impose a commandment upon both the buyer and the seller that the sale not be less than what is stated. And it mentioned both years and harvests to grant the prerogative of the years and the prerogative of the harvests. And it said <b>מִסְפַּר תְּבוּאוֹת</b> and did not say the number of a harvest, to teach that if there were years with no harvest, whether because of blight or the Sabbatical and Jubilee year, they do not count toward his tally." "And one must know the proper reason for this matter: why does HaShem forbid the buyer from returning the field in less than two years etc. — is it forbidden for a person to give away or forgive his property, or to declare it ownerless, and would this be like any other such case? Perhaps it is because HaShem's will is that a person not sell his field, as they interpreted of blessed memory (Torat Kohanim on verse 25): ״כִּי יָמוּךְ אָחִיךָ״ etc. — that a person is not permitted to sell his field and leave the money sitting etc. Therefore HaShem intends, in the command to the buyer not to return the purchase to the seller, that by virtue of this the definitive prospect of return within two years will stand before his eyes, and sometimes he will refrain on account of this and not sell." "<b>Further,</b> it appears based on what the Rambam wrote there in chapter 11, and this is his language: One who sells his field for sixty years — it does not go out in the Jubilee, for what returns in the Jubilee is only something sold unconditionally or in perpetuity, end of quote. And the Kesef Mishneh did not record whence the Rav derived this ruling. Perhaps this ruling is what HaShem recorded in this statement along these lines: <b>בְּמִסְפַּר שָׁנִים אַחַר הַיּוֹבֵל</b> — meaning, when he sells for a number of years, forty or sixty years or more, ״יִמְכָּר״ — meaning, he may sell and the sale may continue even after the Jubilee, and the Jubilee does not interrupt to remove it from his hand since he sold for a number of years. And what it goes on to say, <b>בְּמִסְפַּר שְׁנֵי תְבוּאֹת</b> etc. — meaning, it gives the reason why a sale for years may continue after the Jubilee, and it said ״בְּמִסְפַּר שְׁנֵי תְבוּאֹת יִמְכָּר לָךְ״ — this sale, meaning, since he is selling for years, he is not selling the body of the land itself but rather two harvests, and it is the sale of that which the verse is strict about."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {4 "The phrase וְהוּא שֶׁמַּקְפִּיד הַכָּתוּב is rendered hyper-literally; the syntax is compressed and may be elliptical, but the translation preserves the referential ambiguity intact."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:01:17.586157903Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}